{"title":"2021 Fall AMC 10A Problems/Problem 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(One_Variable)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (One Variable)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Two_Variables)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Two Variables)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Three_Variables)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Three Variables)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Using_the_Boat&#39;s_&quot;Step&quot;)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Using the Boat's \"Step\")</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Relative_Speeds)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Relative Speeds)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_WhyMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by WhyMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_MRENTHUSIASM_(English-Chinese_Bilingual)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by MRENTHUSIASM (English-Chinese Bilingual)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Emily sees a ship traveling at a constant speed along a straight section of a river. She walks parallel to the riverbank at a uniform rate faster than the ship. She counts <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/3/b4328d77715c539955c57ce1aee7b7bc9cdea458.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$210$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> equal steps walking from the back of the ship to the front. Walking in the opposite direction, she counts <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0d6c36fdad9c2874053be46c7e4120f2edcf47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> steps of the same size from the front of the ship to the back. In terms of Emily's equal steps, what is the length of the ship?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/9193ab5f6ba7e16498c6bfbd24987ed03ab955af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }70\\qquad\\textbf{(B) }84\\qquad\\textbf{(C) }98\\qquad\\textbf{(D) }105\\qquad\\textbf{(E) }126$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"419\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28One_Variable.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(One_Variable)\">Solution 1 (One Variable)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (One Variable)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> be the length of the ship.\nThen, in the time that Emily walks <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/3/b4328d77715c539955c57ce1aee7b7bc9cdea458.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$210$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> steps, the ship moves <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/c/e6ca9551a3f71dfb098e5739f3f7cf8a57a9ae79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$210-x$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> steps.\nAlso, in the time that Emily walks <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0d6c36fdad9c2874053be46c7e4120f2edcf47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> steps, the ship moves <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/6/556036c8a3cce0342f67f799cddbaae4c7eb98f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"50\" height=\"12\" /> steps.\nSince the ship and Emily have the same ratio of absolute speeds in either direction, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3adcd8d94741389b07fc0d7a3700c22c1abefe3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{210}{210-x} = \\frac{42}{x-42}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"145\" height=\"40\" />. Dividing both sides by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0d6c36fdad9c2874053be46c7e4120f2edcf47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> and cross multiplying, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/f/acf93f3d94f88958f2bb0c58c92d855e2f14039b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5(x-42) = 210-x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"160\" height=\"20\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/d/6cd0d87a45fb70c3fe5388f6accda72d8ca619b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6x = 420$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"73\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/1/b3109ea253fec69dd0c243bac3625d9f0fff19e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A) }70}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"102\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~ihatemath123\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Two_Variables.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Two_Variables)\">Solution 2 (Two Variables)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Two Variables)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the speed at which Emily walks be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0d6c36fdad9c2874053be46c7e4120f2edcf47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> steps per hour. Let the speed at which the ship is moving be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />. Walking in the direction of the ship, it takes her <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/3/b4328d77715c539955c57ce1aee7b7bc9cdea458.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$210$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> steps, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef15b89777d1a25e0999b0d2c4836887465ac7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {210}{42} = 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"66\" height=\"38\" /> hours, to travel. We can create an equation: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/9/f99a8afe0586d90f69d3bf4165ad7981e4ec4b76.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[d = 5(42-s),\\]\" width=\"109\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the length of the ship. Walking in the opposite direction of the ship, it takes her <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0d6c36fdad9c2874053be46c7e4120f2edcf47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> steps, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/7/957c0f7fa194e66ea09e65c3590f423c2e45a334.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42/42 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"81\" height=\"20\" /> hour. We can create a similar equation: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/0/220e0bbd5517b30af1c6dd943f164daf093e8119.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[d = 1(42+s).\\]\" width=\"109\" height=\"18\" /> Now we have two variables and two equations. We can equate the expressions for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and solve for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/8/508c8de93296ef2d15187bcced0034ab2eb31ecc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} 210-5s &amp;= 42 + s \\\\ s &amp;= 28. \\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"139\" height=\"38\" />\nTherefore, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/7/4571fc10f787b3e1290ec47702001ee7af85300d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d = 42 + s = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A) }70}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"174\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~LucaszDuzMatz (Solution)\n</p><p>~Minor edits by Yvz2900\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Three_Variables.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Three_Variables)\">Solution 3 (Three Variables)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Three Variables)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose that Emily and the ship take steps simultaneously such that Emily's steps cover a greater length than the ship's steps.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be the length of the ship, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be Emily's step length, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be the ship's step length. We wish to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/3/6234f699c3ee362bd8f667bd22c9dd38bb8aeb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac LE.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"25\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>When Emily walks from the back of the ship to the front, she walks a distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/9/179083fdaf770d845a2fd29dac1a229b906cad94.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$210E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and the front of the ship moves a distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/c/95cc224491055cdbae4e80dad627ed3f3a0fa59b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$210S.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/9/5894a421993c38c1f5dfce31d7e0af6b5a0f4d89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$210E=L+210S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"142\" height=\"16\" /> for this scenario, which rearranges to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/b/59b5cddc4d94b49d76d9b47fa67adf012f847229.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[210E-210S=L. \\hspace{15mm}(1)\\]\" width=\"237\" height=\"18\" /> When Emily walks in the opposite direction, she walks a distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/0/cc08bcbdd8b06ca67a7212d925b401fd89350cad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"34\" height=\"14\" /> and the back of the ship moves a distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/b/93b85a0e04d80e38e43818c96957e51f5cea418f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42S.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"36\" height=\"14\" /> We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/0/180a2ce37a3a221f7c19653ef6f54dce8254b7e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42E=L-42S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"124\" height=\"16\" /> for this scenario, which rearranges to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/9/b896b58f1f31864e5b978c4f3f0d378ae7bd2d6b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[42E+42S=L. \\hspace{19.125mm}(2)\\]\" width=\"238\" height=\"18\" />\nWe multiply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/f/62f62a19f0425d8e6121ca0c1cfd3ce96e43c9c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"18\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and then add <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/9/0d9c3b1c142cbd3f8217dabc160c57e4750df241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"18\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/6/b26cd8e0f96e8f0014609318ee6b3d60a1be29ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$420E=6L,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" /> from which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/d/fedf3c4160be9279e559058aa5b4c802e5b73e45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac LE = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A) }70}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"115\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>~Steven Chen (www.professorchenedu.com)\n</p><p>~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Using_the_Boat.27s_.22Step.22.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Using_the_Boat's_&quot;Step&quot;)\">Solution 4 (Using the Boat's \"Step\")</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Using the Boat&#39;s &quot;Step&quot;)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Every time Emily takes a step, the boat also \"takes a step\". Call the length of the boat's step <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />. Call the length of the boat <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>When Emily is walking in the same direction as the boat, every time she takes a step the boat moves an additional distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />. This means that she travels a total distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63c48f1c86ecfc2c915c667edcfbee636d0f488e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + 210 s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"70\" height=\"15\" /> to reach the other end of the boat.\n</p><p>When Emily is walking in the opposite direction of the boat, every time she takes a step the distance till the end of the boat reduces by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> (since the boat is coming towards her and moves a distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />). This means that she travels a total distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/5/11521af2add554830c385d1b8dd70ef0516415e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x - 42 s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"61\" height=\"15\" /> to reach the other end of the boat. \n</p><p>Taking Emily's step as a unit of distance, we now have two equations \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/5/e2561c2ff5cce77d43e4e6ed6fc643d6242accbb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} 210 &amp;= x + 210 s, \\\\ 42 &amp;= x - 42s. \\end{align*}\" width=\"123\" height=\"37\" />\nSolving for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> you get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/c/99cdf7db1c49951a4e42f22c08a0e9c0c5484412.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A) }70}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"67\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~zeeshan12\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Relative_Speeds.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Relative_Speeds)\">Solution 5 (Relative Speeds)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Relative Speeds)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Call the speed of the boat <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/0/7d0220e2a0c12c2a6048f99e473516e1a4efab88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> and the speed of Emily <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4005509f36f6f66504264d8b1efdda11264762d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>Consider the scenario when Emily is walking along with the boat. Relative to an observer on the boat, her speed is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/b/d2bc56772eca7a22bc68ee1b967c0d85c15126b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_e-v_s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>Consider the scenario when Emily is walking in the opposite direction. Relative to an observer on the boat, her speed is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/7/f27295934d03de207c85e547de01998c91116e88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_e+v_s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>Since Emily takes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/3/b4328d77715c539955c57ce1aee7b7bc9cdea458.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$210$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> steps to walk along with the boat and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0d6c36fdad9c2874053be46c7e4120f2edcf47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> steps to walk opposite the boat, that means it takes her <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times longer to walk the length of a stationary boat at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/b/d2bc56772eca7a22bc68ee1b967c0d85c15126b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_e-v_s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"15\" /> compared to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/7/f27295934d03de207c85e547de01998c91116e88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_e+v_s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>This means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/b/2ebc218da3c4bfa7c58daabc060672ffb5739c22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5(v_e-v_s)=v_e+v_s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"157\" height=\"20\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/f/defa3b8998d826e386c4b384e38066efd8b0058c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_s = \\frac{2v_e}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"71\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>As Emily takes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/3/b4328d77715c539955c57ce1aee7b7bc9cdea458.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$210$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> steps to walk the length of the boat at a speed of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/a/7ba775c37a93e410ad4224bc1552797c04423353.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_e- \\frac{2v_e}{3}=\\frac{v_e}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"113\" height=\"38\" />, she must take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/a/ebafcc60a28d8759185d7808c60ea53efc73938e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> of the time to walk the length of the boat at a speed of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4005509f36f6f66504264d8b1efdda11264762d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" />, so our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/0/b4053ceab32d880f42466136fc9f2369e4ef3efb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{210}{3} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A) }70}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"122\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/zq3UPu4nwsE\">https://youtu.be/zq3UPu4nwsE</a>\n</p><p>~IceMatrix\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_WhyMath\">Video Solution by WhyMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by WhyMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/0JOj_fbCB40\">https://youtu.be/0JOj_fbCB40</a>\n</p><p>~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_MRENTHUSIASM_.28English-Chinese_Bilingual.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_MRENTHUSIASM_(English-Chinese_Bilingual)\">Video Solution by MRENTHUSIASM (English-Chinese Bilingual)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by MRENTHUSIASM (English-Chinese Bilingual)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/35UHSd4ZIE0\">https://youtu.be/35UHSd4ZIE0</a>\n</p><p>~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?tit